      "text": "The two screenshots are byte-for-byte identical (matching MD5 hashes), so no further iteration rounds are needed.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe source page (`page.html`) is a minimal fixture with **no author CSS at all** — its appearance is entirely the browser's default UA stylesheet. Its only interesting behavior is JS-driven: an inline `<script>` waits 150ms, then replaces three empty `<div class=\"placeholder\">` nodes inside `#recommendations` with `.product-card` divs (image + name + price), built from a hardcoded `products` array in the script itself.\n\nKey decisions:\n- **Componentized `product-card`**: recognized the repeated card pattern (image, `<p>` name, `<span>` price) as the intentional reusable unit and transcribed it three times using the exact data from the script (Trail Runner Jacket/$128, Insulated Bottle/$32, Camp Lantern/$45), verbatim from source — no invented copy.\n- **Handled the JS-driven section correctly**: rather than cloning the empty placeholder markup (which is all that's in the static HTML), I read the script to determine the actual resolved DOM state and baked that final markup directly into the clone, since that's what a user actually sees.\n- **Asset handling**: the original references `/product.jpg`, a relative path that never resolves even in the live original (this is a local fixture with no real origin/server behind it) — it renders as a broken image with alt text in both. I preserved that reference as-is rather than fabricating a placeholder image, since faithfully reproducing \"broken image, alt text visible\" is the correct match to source behavior, not an unrecreatable gap.\n- **Tailwind usage**: loaded the Tailwind CDN per the required approach, but set `corePlugins.preflight: false` and added no utility classes, since Tailwind's reset would have overridden the very default-UA styling (serif headings, default margins) that this page relies on — applying it would have hurt fidelity, not helped it.\n- **Screenshot comparison**: took a 1280×900 screenshot of the original and of the clone; the two PNGs are byte-identical (same MD5 hash), so one round was sufficient and no further iteration was warranted.\n\nNo parts of the page were unrecreatable — everything needed was fully derivable from the static markup plus the inline script's own logic and data."
